From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 06:42:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] colorspace: the PQ curves of the colour mapping from tables made
 in double precision

The colour mapping (tone and gamut mapping to a display, HDR to SDR)
evaluates SMPTE ST 2084 with pow() at every pixel: the linearization of a
PQ picture, the cone (LMS) values taken to PQ for the tone curve and back,
the brightest channel there and back, the IPT of the gamut mapping and back
to light, and the delinearization to a PQ display. That is 37 pow() of a
component per pixel mapped to SDR and 40 to a PQ display: 74 to 80
exponentials and logarithms, which GPUs run at a quarter of their rate, and
whose precision is each GPU's own (the EOTF in its usual form also cancels
two terms near black).

While the colour mapping runs, sh_pq_eotf() and sh_pq_eotf_inv() now read
PQ from two tables made once in double precision (pq_tables_make()), with
no pow(), exp(), log() or division in the shader:
 - the EOTF, 256 x 32 RGBA32F: a quadratic through the ends and middle of
   each cell of 1/4096 of [2^-6, 1] and of 1/256 of each octave of
   [2^-22, 2^-6) (the cell taken from the bits of the float), 0 below 2^-22;
 - the inverse EOTF, 256 x 64 RGBA32F: a cell for each 1/128 of an octave of
   [2^-126, 2), its value at the start held as two floats (hi + lo) and a
   quadratic for the rest; 0 (and what a GPU flushes to 0) takes the value
   of 0, and from 2 on (20000 cd/m^2) pow() as before.
Where the GPU cannot make them (no sampleable 32-bit float texture, or a
GLSL without texelFetch() and floatBitsToUint()), PQ is computed with pow()
as before, and the log says why, once. pl_renderer_pq_tables() tells which
the colour mapping of a renderer takes.

Measured on Mesa's software OpenGL ES (llvmpipe through zink), the curves
as the GPU evaluates them against ST 2084 in double precision, over every
16-bit code and 6.3 million samples a function (dense uniform and
log-uniform over the domain the shaders feed them):
 - EOTF, the error of the output taken back to 12-bit PQ codes: with pow()
   at most 0.0537 (mean 0.0087) from 0.5 up, 0.0238 (0.0039) from 2^-6,
   0.0011 below; from the tables at most 5.2e-5 (mean 1.2e-5), 4.6e-5 and
   7.3e-6. Relative error of Y: 1.25e-4 against 1.2e-7.
 - Inverse EOTF, in 12-bit codes: with pow() at most 0.0855 (mean 0.0134)
   from 0.01 up, 0.0389 from 1e-6, 0.0014 below; from the tables at most
   2.5e-4 (mean 8.8e-5), 1.2e-4 and 4.0e-6. At 0 both give its value; from 2
   on both pow().
Every region more precise, by 200 to 1000 times. The pow(x, 1/2.4) of an
SDR target is not replaced (within 7e-5 of an 8-bit code).

Where a mapped picture changes, it is at the edge of the target gamut,
where the gamut mapping leaves a channel at or near 0: the LMS-to-RGB
matrix cancels terms thousands to millions of times larger than that
channel, and the gamma and PQ curves are steep near 0, so an error of
1e-4 in a cone becomes up to 9 codes of 255 to SDR, or 205 of 4095 to a
PQ display, in a channel of less than 0.1 cd/m^2 beside others of 100 to
800 cd/m^2. Between before and after, over every changed pixel of 19
pictures and charts, at most 0.072 delta E ITP (ITU-R BT.2124). Read back
after the gamut mapping and taken to the output in double precision, the
last part of the chain is exact with the tables (at most 7 codes of
4095, 0.14 on average, in the picture where it cancels most) and was up to
150 codes off with pow(). Moving each
PQ call by the error its table may have bounds how far the new picture is
from the same mapping with exact curves: of the 14.3 million samples that
changed, 13.1 million are provably nearer to it with the tables, and the
rest lie within that bound, which float arithmetic cannot settle.
---
diff --git a/src/include/libplacebo/renderer.h b/src/include/libplacebo/renderer.h
